INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','easy','mcq','How many lobes does the right lung contain?',''["A. Two", "B. Four", "C. Three", "D. Five"]','C','The right lung is divided into three lobes — superior, middle, and inferior — separated by the horizontal and oblique fissures. The left lung has only two lobes due to the cardiac notch.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','easy','mcq','Which cell type produces pulmonary surfactant?','["A. Type I pneumocytes","B. Type II pneumocytes","C. Club (Clara) cells","D. Alveolar macrophages"]','B','Type II pneumocytes (granular pneumocytes) synthesise and secrete surfactant, a phospholipid-rich mixture that reduces alveolar surface tension. Type I pneumocytes are thin cells responsible for gas exchange.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','medium','mcq','Which cell type lines the majority (~97%) of the alveolar surface area and is primarily responsible for gas exchange?',''["A. Type II pneumocytes", "B. Alveolar macrophages", "C. Club (Clara) cells", "D. Type I pneumocytes"]','D','Type I pneumocytes are flat squamous cells covering approximately 97% of the alveolar surface. Their extreme thinness (~0.1–0.5 µm) minimises diffusion distance for gas exchange. Type II cells cover only ~3% but are metabolically far more active.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','medium','mcq','Which structure marks the boundary between the conducting zone and the respiratory zone of the lung?','["A. Terminal bronchiole","B. Respiratory bronchiole","C. Alveolar duct","D. Alveolar sac"]','B','The respiratory bronchiole is the first airway that contains alveoli in its wall and therefore participates in gas exchange. All airway generations proximal to it — including the terminal bronchiole — form the purely conducting zone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','medium','mcq','The right main bronchus is more prone to foreign body aspiration than the left primarily because it is:',''["A. Narrower and more horizontal", "B. Wider and more vertical", "C. Longer with more cartilaginous rings", "D. Lined with non-ciliated epithelium"]','B','The right main bronchus diverges at a smaller angle (~25°) from the trachea, making it more vertical and wider than the left (~45°). This anatomy makes inhaled foreign bodies more likely to enter the right lung.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','medium','mcq','How many bronchopulmonary segments does the right lung contain?','["A. 8","B. 9","C. 10","D. 12"]','C','The right lung contains 10 bronchopulmonary segments: 3 in the upper lobe, 2 in the middle lobe, and 5 in the lower lobe. Each segment is supplied by its own segmental bronchus and pulmonary arterial branch, making them surgically resectable units.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','medium','mcq','Approximately at which generation of the airway tree do cartilaginous rings disappear, marking the transition to bronchioles?',''["A. Generation 4", "B. Generation 11", "C. Generation 8", "D. Generation 16"]','B','Bronchioles begin at approximately generation 11 of the airway tree where cartilage is absent. They are maintained patent by radial traction from surrounding alveolar tissue rather than cartilaginous support. Loss of this traction (e.g., emphysema) causes small airway collapse.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','hard','mcq','A patient undergoes surgical resection of the lingula. Which lobe and segments of the left lung are removed?',''["A. Left lower lobe, anterior basal and lateral basal segments", "B. Left upper lobe, apicoposterior and anterior segments", "C. Left upper lobe, superior and inferior lingular segments", "D. Left lower lobe, superior segment only"]','C','The lingula is a tongue-like projection of the left upper lobe anatomically equivalent to the right middle lobe. It consists of two bronchopulmonary segments: superior lingular (B4) and inferior lingular (B5).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','hard','mcq','Club (Clara) cells, found predominantly in terminal and respiratory bronchioles, primarily serve which function?',''["A. Mucociliary clearance of inhaled particles", "B. Phagocytosis of alveolar debris", "C. Production of secretory IgA for mucosal immunity", "D. Secretion of a protective lipid-rich material and cytochrome P450-mediated detoxification of inhaled toxins"]','D','Club cells are non-ciliated secretory cells that produce a lipid-protein secretion protecting the bronchiolar epithelium. They also contain cytochrome P450 enzymes that detoxify inhaled xenobiotics, and they serve as progenitor cells for bronchiolar epithelium repair.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Anatomy & Airway Structure','hard','mcq','Pores of Kohn are inter-alveolar communications that become clinically significant when they allow:',''["A. Surfactant to redistribute between adjacent alveoli", "B. Transfer of alveolar macrophages between alveoli", "C. Collateral ventilation, which can maintain aeration of lung distal to an obstructed airway", "D. Spread of pulmonary oedema fluid to adjacent alveoli"]','C','Pores of Kohn permit collateral ventilation between adjacent alveoli and, via the canals of Lambert, between bronchioles and alveoli. This prevents atelectasis when a small airway is blocked, but also allows spread of infection across a lobe (e.g., pneumococcal lobar pneumonia).','universal',false,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','easy','mcq','Normal total lung compliance in a healthy adult is approximately:',''["A. 50 ml/cm H\u2082O", "B. 200 ml/cm H\u2082O", "C. 100 ml/cm H\u2082O", "D. 400 ml/cm H\u2082O"]','B','Normal total lung compliance is approximately 200 ml/cm H₂O, meaning the lungs expand by 200 ml for every 1 cm H₂O increase in transpulmonary pressure. Reduced compliance occurs in pulmonary fibrosis; increased compliance in emphysema.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','easy','mcq','According to Laplace''s law, the pressure required to keep an alveolus open is:',''["A. Directly proportional to both surface tension and radius", "B. Inversely proportional to both surface tension and radius", "C. Directly proportional to surface tension and inversely proportional to radius", "D. Independent of surface tension"]','C','Laplace''s law: P = 2T/r. Pressure is proportional to surface tension (T) and inversely proportional to radius (r). Smaller alveoli therefore tend to collapse unless surfactant reduces their surface tension more than larger alveoli, stabilising the system.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','medium','mcq','Which phospholipid is the most abundant and functionally important component of pulmonary surfactant?',''["A. Phosphatidylglycerol", "B. Phosphatidylinositol", "C. Sphingomyelin", "D. Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC)"]','D','Dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine (DPPC, also called dipalmitoyl lecithin) constitutes approximately 40–50% of surfactant and is the principal surface-tension-lowering molecule. Surfactant production begins at about 26–28 weeks gestation, explaining respiratory distress syndrome in premature neonates.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','medium','mcq','At FRC, breathing muscles are relaxed. The equilibrium is maintained because:',''["A. Inward elastic recoil of lungs equals outward elastic recoil of the chest wall", "B. Inspiratory muscle force equals expiratory muscle force", "C. Diaphragm contraction balances abdominal pressure", "D. Alveolar pressure equals atmospheric pressure"]','A','At FRC, no respiratory muscles are active. The inward elastic recoil of the lungs (tendency to collapse) equals the outward elastic recoil of the chest wall (tendency to spring out). These opposing forces balance, defining the resting end-expiratory lung volume.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','medium','mcq','Transpulmonary pressure is defined as:',''["A. Alveolar pressure minus pleural pressure", "B. Atmospheric pressure minus alveolar pressure", "C. Pleural pressure minus atmospheric pressure", "D. Alveolar pressure minus atmospheric pressure"]','A','Transpulmonary pressure = Palveolar − Ppleural. It is the distending pressure that keeps the lung open. At end-expiration (FRC), Palv = 0 cmH₂O (atmospheric), Ppleural = −5 cmH₂O, so transpulmonary pressure = +5 cmH₂O, preventing lung collapse.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','medium','mcq','A patient with pulmonary fibrosis has decreased lung compliance. Which spirometric pattern is expected?','["A. Increased TLC with decreased FEV1/FVC","B. Decreased TLC with normal or increased FEV1/FVC","C. Normal TLC with severely decreased FEV1 only","D. Increased RV with decreased VC"]','B','Pulmonary fibrosis is restrictive. Stiff (low-compliance) lungs reduce all volumes — TLC, VC, FRC, and RV. Because airways are not intrinsically obstructed, FEV1 and FVC fall proportionally, so FEV1/FVC is normal or increased (>0.80).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','medium','mcq','During forced expiration, dynamic airway compression occurs at the equal pressure point because:','["A. Increased positive pleural pressure compresses airways at the point where luminal pressure equals pleural pressure","B. The diaphragm descends and narrows the bronchi","C. Surfactant is depleted during forced exhalation","D. Pulmonary vascular pressure rises and compresses bronchi externally"]','A','During forced expiration, positive pleural pressure is transmitted to airways. At the equal pressure point, airway luminal pressure = pleural pressure; downstream (mouthward), pleural pressure exceeds luminal pressure, collapsing airways. This creates effort-independent maximum expiratory flow — the hallmark of obstructive disease.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','hard','mcq','A premature neonate at 28 weeks gestation develops progressive respiratory distress shortly after birth. The primary pathophysiological mechanism is:',''["A. Surfactant deficiency causing alveolar collapse and markedly reduced compliance", "B. Airway obstruction by meconium", "C. Excess surfactant production causing alveolar flooding", "D. Immature medullary respiratory centres causing central apnoea"]','A','Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (NRDS) results from surfactant deficiency; type II pneumocytes are functionally immature before ~35 weeks gestation. Without surfactant, high surface tension causes progressive atelectasis and the infant must generate enormous inspiratory pressures. Treatment is exogenous surfactant and antenatal glucocorticoids.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','hard','mcq','Hysteresis in the lung pressure-volume curve means:',''["A. Greater pressure is required to inflate the lung to a given volume than to maintain that volume during deflation, due to surface tension forces", "B. Compliance is identical during inspiration and expiration", "C. Airway resistance increases during expiration only", "D. Tidal volume is identical on inspiration and expiration"]','A','Hysteresis occurs because during inspiration, pressure must overcome elastic recoil AND surface tension to recruit collapsed alveoli. During expiration, surfactant compresses and further reduces surface tension, so less pressure is needed. The inspiratory and expiratory pressure-volume curves trace different paths.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Pulmonary Mechanics','hard','mcq','In emphysema, static compliance is increased but expiratory flow is severely reduced. The mechanism of airflow obstruction is:','["A. Bronchoconstriction from airway inflammation","B. Loss of alveolar radial traction that normally holds small airways open during expiration","C. Mucus plugging of large central airways","D. Increased surface tension from surfactant destruction"]','B','In emphysema, destruction of alveolar walls removes the radial traction that normally holds small airways open. During expiration (especially forced), these unsupported airways collapse prematurely, trapping air and increasing RV. FEV1 is reduced disproportionately, giving low FEV1/FVC despite high static compliance.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','easy','mcq','Which lung volume CANNOT be measured directly by spirometry?','["A. Tidal volume","B. Inspiratory reserve volume","C. Residual volume","D. Expiratory reserve volume"]','C','Residual volume (RV) is the air remaining after maximal expiration — it cannot be expelled into the spirometer. It requires indirect methods such as helium dilution, nitrogen washout, or body plethysmography. Any capacity containing RV (FRC, TLC) also cannot be measured by spirometry alone.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','easy','mcq','Normal tidal volume in a healthy adult male at rest is approximately:',''["A. 150 ml", "B. 500 ml", "C. 350 ml", "D. 1200 ml"]','B','Tidal volume (Vt) averages ~500 ml (0.5 L) in a healthy adult male at rest. This is the volume moved with each normal effortless breath. At 12–16 breaths/min, this gives a minute ventilation of 6–8 L/min.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','medium','mcq','Total Lung Capacity (TLC) in a healthy young adult male averages approximately:',''["A. 5800 ml", "B. 3500 ml", "C. 4600 ml", "D. 7000 ml"]','A','TLC = VC + RV ≈ 4600 + 1200 = 5800 ml in a healthy young adult male (Guyton & Hall values). TLC is increased in emphysema (air trapping) and decreased in restrictive diseases such as fibrosis or pleural effusion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','medium','mcq','A patient has a FEV1 of 1.2 L and an FVC of 3.0 L (FEV1/FVC = 0.40). This pattern is most consistent with:',''["A. Obstructive lung disease (e.g., COPD or asthma)", "B. Restrictive lung disease (e.g., pulmonary fibrosis)", "C. Normal spirometry", "D. Mixed obstructive-restrictive pattern with normal ratio"]','A','FEV1/FVC < 0.70 defines airflow obstruction. Here the ratio is 0.40, indicating severe obstruction. In obstructive disease, FEV1 falls disproportionately more than FVC because airways collapse during forced expiration. Normal FEV1/FVC ≥ 0.80.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','medium','mcq','Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) equals:',''["A. Expiratory reserve volume + Residual volume", "B. Tidal volume + Inspiratory reserve volume", "C. Vital capacity + Residual volume", "D. Inspiratory capacity + Expiratory reserve volume"]','A','FRC = ERV + RV. It is the volume remaining in the lungs after a normal passive expiration. In healthy adult males, FRC ≈ 1100 + 1200 = 2300 ml. FRC increases in emphysema (hyperinflation) and decreases in the supine position, obesity, and restrictive diseases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','medium','mcq','On a flow-volume loop, obstructive disease characteristically shows which expiratory pattern?','["A. A tall narrow peak with steep linear descent","B. A scooped-out (concave) expiratory curve with reduced mid and late expiratory flows","C. A vertical expiratory limb with increased FVC","D. No change from the normal pattern"]','B','In obstructive disease (asthma, COPD), the expiratory limb shows a classic scooped or concave shape due to disproportionate reduction in flow at low lung volumes. Peak expiratory flow may be relatively preserved initially, but FEF25–75% is markedly reduced.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','medium','mcq','Inspiratory Capacity (IC) is equal to:','["A. Tidal volume + Inspiratory reserve volume only","B. TLC − FRC only","C. The maximal volume inspireable from FRC only","D. All of the above — these are equivalent definitions"]','D','IC = Vt + IRV = TLC − FRC = maximum volume inspireable from FRC. In a healthy adult male, IC ≈ 500 + 3000 = 3500 ml. IC is reduced in restrictive disease and hyperinflation (elevated FRC reduces IC because TLC cannot increase proportionally).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','hard','mcq','Body plethysmography measures TLC using Boyle''s law. Compared with helium dilution, plethysmographic TLC in a patient with severe COPD and bullae will be:',''["A. Lower, because gas cannot exit the bullae during helium equilibration", "B. Identical, since both methods measure total lung gas", "C. Higher, because plethysmography measures all intrathoracic gas including trapped air in bullae", "D. Lower, because plethysmography underestimates large lung volumes"]','C','Helium dilution measures only gas communicating with the airways; trapped gas in bullae is excluded, giving a falsely low TLC/FRC. Body plethysmography measures total thoracic gas volume (by Boyle''s law using pressure-volume changes during panting) and therefore includes trapped gas, giving a larger, more accurate value.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','hard','mcq','In a patient with a large right pleural effusion, which spirometric pattern is most expected?',''["A. Decreased FEV1/FVC with normal TLC \u2014 obstructive pattern", "B. Increased TLC with decreased FVC", "C. Normal spirometry with increased RV", "D. Decreased FVC and decreased TLC with normal FEV1/FVC \u2014 restrictive pattern"]','D','A large pleural effusion compresses the lung externally, reducing FRC, VC, and TLC — a restrictive defect. Airways are not intrinsically obstructed, so FEV1 and FVC fall proportionally, maintaining or increasing FEV1/FVC ratio.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Lung Volumes & Capacities','hard','mcq','Closing capacity (CC) exceeds FRC in elderly and obese patients. The physiological consequence is:','["A. Improved V/Q matching in the lung bases","B. Small airway closure during tidal breathing, causing gas trapping and V/Q mismatch with resultant hypoxaemia","C. Decreased airway resistance throughout the respiratory cycle","D. Reduced work of breathing due to early airway closure"]','B','When CC > FRC, small airways close during normal tidal breathing, trapping gas distally and creating V/Q mismatch with consequent hypoxaemia. This explains why the elderly and obese have lower PaO₂, particularly when supine (FRC decreases further in the supine position).','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','easy','mcq','Anatomical dead space in a healthy young adult normally measures approximately:',''["A. 150 ml", "B. 50 ml", "C. 350 ml", "D. 500 ml"]','A','Anatomical dead space is ~150 ml in a young adult male — approximately 1 ml per pound (2.2 ml/kg) body weight. It represents the volume of conducting airways (nose to terminal bronchioles) that contain fresh air but do not participate in gas exchange.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','easy','mcq','Alveolar ventilation (VA) is calculated as:',''["A. Tidal volume \u00d7 Respiratory rate", "B. Minute ventilation + Dead space ventilation", "C. Tidal volume / Respiratory rate", "D. (Tidal volume \u2212 Dead space volume) \u00d7 Respiratory rate"]','D','Alveolar ventilation = (Vt − VD) × RR. Only the portion of each breath reaching the alveoli participates in gas exchange. Example: (500 ml − 150 ml) × 12 = 4200 ml/min alveolar ventilation. Minute ventilation overestimates effective ventilation by including dead space.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','medium','mcq','In the clinical application of the Bohr equation for physiological dead space, what is used as the surrogate for alveolar PCO₂?',''["A. Mixed expired O\u2082 tension", "B. End-tidal CO\u2082 tension alone", "C. Venous PCO\u2082", "D. Arterial PCO\u2082 (PaCO\u2082)"]','D','Bohr equation: VD/Vt = (PaCO₂ − PeCO₂) / PaCO₂. Since all CO₂ in expired gas comes from perfused alveoli, arterial PCO₂ equals ideal alveolar PCO₂. In normal lungs, physiological dead space ≈ anatomical dead space; it increases with V/Q mismatch (e.g., pulmonary embolism).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','medium','mcq','A patient breathes at 20 breaths/min with a tidal volume of 200 ml and a dead space of 150 ml. What is the alveolar ventilation rate?',''["A. 4000 ml/min", "B. 2600 ml/min", "C. 3400 ml/min", "D. 1000 ml/min"]','D','Alveolar ventilation = (Vt − VD) × RR = (200 − 150) × 20 = 50 × 20 = 1000 ml/min. This rapid shallow breathing pattern is very inefficient — nearly all of the minute ventilation (4000 ml/min) is wasted ventilating dead space. This pattern leads to CO₂ retention.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','medium','mcq','Which condition increases physiological dead space ABOVE anatomical dead space?',''["A. Hypoventilation", "B. Pneumonia causing lung consolidation", "C. Pulmonary embolism", "D. Bronchospasm"]','C','Physiological dead space = anatomical dead space + alveolar dead space. Alveolar dead space arises when alveoli are ventilated but not perfused (high V/Q units). Pulmonary embolism blocks perfusion to ventilated lung units, creating alveolar dead space and increasing physiological dead space. PaCO₂ rises or ventilation must increase to compensate.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','medium','mcq','Alveolar PCO₂ (PACO₂) is related to alveolar ventilation (VA) and CO₂ production (VCO₂) as follows:',''["A. PACO\u2082 is directly proportional to VCO\u2082 and inversely proportional to alveolar ventilation", "B. PACO\u2082 is directly proportional to both VCO\u2082 and alveolar ventilation", "C. PACO\u2082 is inversely proportional to VCO\u2082 and directly proportional to minute ventilation", "D. PACO\u2082 is independent of alveolar ventilation"]','A','PACO₂ = (VCO₂ / VA) × k. When CO₂ production increases (exercise, fever) or alveolar ventilation decreases (hypoventilation, sedation), PACO₂ rises. This is the basis of the alveolar ventilation equation and explains hypercapnia in respiratory failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','medium','mcq','A mechanically ventilated patient has PaCO₂ of 55 mmHg. The most appropriate ventilator adjustment to lower PaCO₂ is:',''["A. Decrease tidal volume to 300 ml", "B. Add PEEP to recruit alveoli", "C. Increase respiratory rate or tidal volume to increase alveolar ventilation", "D. Reduce FiO\u2082"]','C','PaCO₂ is inversely proportional to alveolar ventilation. To lower PaCO₂, increase alveolar ventilation by increasing RR or Vt (increasing Vt is more efficient since dead space proportion decreases). PEEP recruits alveoli to improve oxygenation, not CO₂ clearance directly.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','hard','mcq','The normal whole-lung V/Q ratio is approximately 0.8. This means:',''["A. Both A and B describe the same ratio", "B. For every litre of ventilation there are 0.8 litres of perfusion", "C. Alveolar ventilation (~4 L/min) is slightly less than pulmonary blood flow/cardiac output (~5 L/min)", "D. Ventilation exceeds perfusion, giving a ratio greater than 1"]','A','Normal resting alveolar ventilation ≈ 4 L/min; cardiac output (pulmonary blood flow) ≈ 5 L/min; V/Q = 4/5 = 0.8. The lung is slightly over-perfused relative to ventilation, contributing to the normal alveolar-arterial PO₂ gradient.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','hard','mcq','A patient receives a tracheostomy, reducing anatomical dead space from 150 ml to 50 ml. With unchanged respiratory pattern (Vt 500 ml, RR 12/min), alveolar ventilation increases by:','["A. 600 ml/min","B. 1200 ml/min","C. 2400 ml/min","D. No change — dead space reduction does not affect alveolar ventilation"]','B','Original VA = (500 − 150) × 12 = 4200 ml/min. Post-tracheostomy VA = (500 − 50) × 12 = 5400 ml/min. Increase = 1200 ml/min. Reducing dead space improves alveolar ventilation efficiency — important in patients with borderline ventilatory reserve.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Ventilation & Dead Space','hard','mcq','Using the alveolar gas equation (PAO₂ = PiO₂ − PACO₂/R) at sea level on room air (PB=760, PH₂O=47 mmHg, FiO₂=0.21, PaCO₂=40 mmHg, R=0.8), the expected PAO₂ is closest to:','["A. 100 mmHg","B. 150 mmHg","C. 40 mmHg","D. 60 mmHg"]','A','PiO₂ = 0.21 × (760 − 47) = 0.21 × 713 = 149.7 mmHg. PAO₂ = 149.7 − (40/0.8) = 149.7 − 50 = 99.7 ≈ 100 mmHg. Comparing this to arterial PO₂ (~95 mmHg) gives the normal A-a gradient of ~5–10 mmHg.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','easy','mcq','According to Fick''s law of diffusion, the rate of gas transfer across the alveolar membrane is INVERSELY proportional to:',''["A. Membrane thickness", "B. Surface area of the membrane", "C. Partial pressure difference across the membrane", "D. Solubility of the gas"]','A','Fick''s law: Rate ∝ (A × D × ΔP) / T, where A = surface area, D = diffusivity, ΔP = pressure gradient, T = thickness. Diffusion rate is INVERSELY proportional to thickness. Increased thickness in pulmonary oedema or fibrosis reduces gas exchange.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','easy','mcq','Carbon dioxide diffuses across the respiratory membrane approximately how many times faster than oxygen?',''["A. The same rate", "B. 2 times faster", "C. 100 times faster", "D. 20 times faster"]','D','CO₂ diffuses ~20 times faster than O₂ across the respiratory membrane primarily due to its much higher solubility in tissue fluids, even though its molecular weight is slightly higher. This is why CO₂ exchange is rarely impaired by diffusion limitation, unlike O₂.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','medium','mcq','A patient with interstitial pulmonary fibrosis has reduced DLCO. Exercise-induced hypoxaemia occurs because:',''["A. Increased CO\u2082 production drives O\u2082 off haemoglobin via the Bohr effect", "B. Increased cardiac output causes pulmonary oedema during exercise", "C. Ventilatory failure causes hypoventilation during exercise", "D. Reduced surface area and thickened membrane limit O\u2082 diffusion, worsened when capillary transit time shortens during exercise"]','D','In fibrosis, the diffusion capacity (DLCO) is reduced due to thickened membrane and reduced surface area. At rest, 0.75-second capillary transit time is sufficient for O₂ equilibration. During exercise, transit time shortens to ~0.25 seconds — insufficient for O₂ equilibration through the thickened membrane — causing diffusion-limited hypoxaemia.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','medium','mcq','Which gas is perfusion-limited (rather than diffusion-limited) under normal resting conditions?',''["A. Nitrous oxide (N\u2082O)", "B. Carbon monoxide (CO)", "C. Oxygen at high altitude", "D. Oxygen in pulmonary fibrosis"]','A','Nitrous oxide (N₂O) is perfusion-limited because it equilibrates so rapidly across the alveolar membrane that its uptake depends entirely on blood flow. CO is diffusion-limited because it binds avidly to haemoglobin, maintaining near-zero blood PCO and a persistent gradient. O₂ is normally perfusion-limited but becomes diffusion-limited in fibrosis or during heavy exercise.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','medium','mcq','The normal diffusing capacity for O₂ (DLO₂) at rest in a young adult male is approximately:',''["A. 5 ml/min/mmHg", "B. 60 ml/min/mmHg", "C. 21 ml/min/mmHg", "D. 100 ml/min/mmHg"]','C','Resting DLO₂ ≈ 21 ml/min/mmHg (Guyton & Hall). During strenuous exercise it can rise to ~65 ml/min/mmHg due to recruitment of additional pulmonary capillaries and improved V/Q matching. DLCO (measured clinically) is ~17–25 ml/min/mmHg at rest.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','medium','mcq','True intrapulmonary shunt (V/Q = 0) differs from V/Q mismatch in that shunt:',''["A. Is corrected by administering 100% oxygen", "B. Only occurs with anatomical cardiac defects such as ASD", "C. Cannot be corrected by 100% oxygen because shunted blood bypasses ventilated alveoli entirely", "D. Causes hypercapnia more than hypoxaemia"]','C','True shunt (V/Q = 0) bypasses ventilated alveoli entirely, so no amount of supplemental oxygen can oxygenate the shunted blood. In V/Q mismatch (low but non-zero V/Q), supplemental O₂ can oxygenate poorly ventilated units. Failure to correct hypoxaemia with high-flow O₂ suggests shunt physiology.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','medium','mcq','The normal alveolar-arterial (A-a) PO₂ gradient on room air in a healthy young adult is:','["A. 0 mmHg","B. 5–15 mmHg","C. 40 mmHg","D. Greater than 20 mmHg always"]','B','The normal A-a gradient is 5–15 mmHg, arising from ~2% anatomical shunt (bronchial and Thebesian veins) and minor V/Q inequality. It increases with age (normal ≤ [age/4 + 4] mmHg) and is elevated in all forms of intrinsic lung disease (shunt, V/Q mismatch, or diffusion impairment).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','hard','mcq','A patient has an elevated A-a gradient that fully corrects with 100% O₂ inhalation. The most likely cause is:',''["A. V/Q mismatch (e.g., pulmonary embolism or COPD)", "B. Intracardiac right-to-left shunt (e.g., patent foramen ovale)", "C. Pulmonary arteriovenous malformation", "D. Diffusion impairment alone without any shunt"]','A','When the A-a gradient corrects fully with 100% O₂, it indicates low but non-zero V/Q units that can be recruited with supplemental O₂. True shunts (anatomical or intrapulmonary) do NOT correct with 100% O₂. The 100% O₂ test distinguishes V/Q mismatch from shunt physiology.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','hard','mcq','DLCO is measured using carbon monoxide because:',''["A. CO has the highest solubility of all gases", "B. CO is non-toxic in the concentrations used", "C. CO does not react with haemoglobin and stays dissolved", "D. CO binds haemoglobin with ~240\u00d7 greater affinity than O\u2082, keeping capillary PCO near zero, making CO uptake purely diffusion-limited"]','D','CO binds haemoglobin with ~240 times the affinity of O₂, maintaining near-zero dissolved CO in blood and a persistent driving gradient. This makes CO uptake purely diffusion-limited, so DLCO = (CO uptake rate) / (alveolar PCO − capillary PCO) measures membrane conductance.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Gas Exchange & Diffusion','hard','mcq','A 35-year-old with scleroderma has PaO₂ 68 mmHg at rest and PaO₂ 48 mmHg on exercise (room air). DLCO is 40% predicted. The mechanism most responsible for exercise desaturation is:',''["A. Increased O\u2082 consumption overwhelms cardiac output", "B. V/Q mismatch from exercise-induced hypoxic vasoconstriction", "C. Diffusion impairment with shortened pulmonary capillary transit time during exercise", "D. CO\u2082 retention causing the Haldane effect"]','C','Scleroderma causes interstitial lung disease with reduced DLCO due to thickened diffusion membrane and reduced surface area. Exercise increases cardiac output, shortening pulmonary capillary transit time from ~0.75 s to ~0.25 s. The already-impaired membrane cannot achieve O₂ equilibration, causing diffusion-limited exercise desaturation.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','easy','mcq','Normal haemoglobin oxygen-carrying capacity (Hüfner''s constant) is approximately:',''["A. 0.5 ml O\u2082/g Hb", "B. 2.0 ml O\u2082/g Hb", "C. 3.5 ml O\u2082/g Hb", "D. 1.34 ml O\u2082/g Hb"]','D','Each gram of haemoglobin can bind 1.34 ml of O₂ when fully saturated (Hüfner''s constant). With normal Hb of 15 g/dl, O₂-carrying capacity = 15 × 1.34 = 20.1 ml/dl. This Hb-bound O₂ dominates transport; dissolved O₂ contributes only ~0.3 ml/dl.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','easy','mcq','The oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the RIGHT in response to:','["A. Decreased temperature","B. Decreased PCO₂","C. Increased 2,3-DPG","D. Alkalosis (increased pH)"]','C','Increased 2,3-DPG, increased PCO₂, increased H⁺ (acidosis), and increased temperature all shift the O₂-Hb curve rightward (Bohr effect), reducing Hb affinity for O₂ and promoting O₂ offloading to tissues. Decreased temperature, alkalosis, and decreased PCO₂ shift the curve leftward.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','medium','mcq','The P50 of haemoglobin refers to:',''["A. The PO\u2082 in arterial blood under normal conditions", "B. The maximum PO\u2082 that haemoglobin can bind", "C. The PO\u2082 at which haemoglobin is 50% saturated under standard conditions", "D. The PO\u2082 at which cardiac output is 50% of maximum"]','C','P50 is the PO₂ at which Hb is 50% saturated at standard conditions (pH 7.4, 37°C, PCO₂ 40 mmHg). Normal P50 ≈ 26–27 mmHg. A rightward shift increases P50 (lower affinity, better tissue O₂ delivery). Foetal Hb (HbF) has a lower P50 (~20 mmHg) indicating higher O₂ affinity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','medium','mcq','Carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning causes tissue hypoxia by which primary mechanism?',''["A. CO binds cytochrome oxidase, blocking cellular respiration", "B. CO causes bronchospasm, reducing alveolar ventilation", "C. CO reacts with surfactant, causing alveolar collapse", "D. CO displaces O\u2082 from Hb with 240\u00d7 affinity and shifts the O\u2082-Hb curve leftward, impairing O\u2082 delivery"]','D','CO binds Hb with ~240 times the affinity of O₂, forming carboxyhaemoglobin (COHb). This reduces available Hb binding sites AND shifts the O₂-Hb curve leftward (increased affinity for remaining O₂), severely impairing O₂ unloading at tissues. PaO₂ may be normal; SpO₂ on pulse oximetry is falsely normal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','medium','mcq','2,3-Diphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG) production by red blood cells is increased in:',''["A. Anaemia and chronic hypoxaemia", "B. Storage of blood in blood banks", "C. Hypothyroidism", "D. Alkalosis alone"]','A','2,3-DPG is produced by RBCs in the Rapoport-Luebering shunt of glycolysis. Production increases with anaemia, chronic hypoxaemia (altitude, COPD), and hyperthyroidism — situations requiring enhanced tissue O₂ delivery. 2,3-DPG decreases during blood storage, reducing O₂-releasing capacity of banked blood.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','medium','mcq','The Bohr effect describes:',''["A. Increased Hb O\u2082 affinity in the presence of CO\u2082 and H\u207a, promoting O\u2082 loading in the lungs", "B. Increased O\u2082 solubility at high partial pressures", "C. Decreased Hb O\u2082 affinity in the presence of CO\u2082 and H\u207a, promoting O\u2082 unloading in tissues", "D. The relationship between CO\u2082 and bicarbonate formation"]','C','The Bohr effect: rising PCO₂ and falling pH in metabolically active tissues shift the O₂-Hb curve rightward, facilitating O₂ release. In pulmonary capillaries, CO₂ leaves and pH rises, shifting the curve leftward — promoting O₂ loading. This bidirectional benefit is a key physiological adaptation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','medium','mcq','Arterial oxygen content (CaO₂) is calculated as:',''["A. CaO\u2082 = 1.34 \u00d7 Hb \u00d7 SaO\u2082 only", "B. CaO\u2082 = PaO\u2082 / SaO\u2082", "C. CaO\u2082 = (1.34 \u00d7 Hb \u00d7 SaO\u2082) + (0.003 \u00d7 PaO\u2082)", "D. CaO\u2082 = Cardiac output \u00d7 PaO\u2082"]','C','CaO₂ = (1.34 × Hb × SaO₂) + (0.003 × PaO₂). The first term is Hb-bound O₂; the second is dissolved O₂. At normal values: (1.34 × 15 × 0.98) + (0.003 × 95) ≈ 19.7 + 0.3 ≈ 20 ml/dl. Dissolved O₂ becomes important only during hyperbaric O₂ therapy.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','hard','mcq','Foetal haemoglobin (HbF) has higher O₂ affinity than adult HbA. The molecular basis is:','["A. HbF contains alpha and gamma chains; gamma chains bind 2,3-DPG less avidly than beta chains in HbA","B. HbF contains beta and delta chains with increased cooperative binding","C. HbF is smaller and can carry more O₂ per molecule","D. HbF has additional iron atoms in its haem groups"]','A','HbF = 2 alpha + 2 gamma chains (vs. 2 alpha + 2 beta in HbA). The gamma chains have lower affinity for 2,3-DPG (which normally stabilises the deoxy T-state by binding the beta-chain cleft). Less 2,3-DPG binding means HbF stays in the higher-affinity oxy-R state more readily — lower P50 (~20 mmHg vs. 27 mmHg for HbA).','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','hard','mcq','Methaemoglobinaemia causes hypoxia by which mechanism?',''["A. Inhibition of cytochrome oxidase in mitochondria", "B. Competitive displacement of O\u2082 by nitric oxide", "C. Conversion of Fe\u00b2\u207a to Fe\u00b3\u207a, which cannot bind O\u2082, and a leftward shift of the O\u2082-Hb curve in remaining normal subunits", "D. Oxidative haemolysis destroying red blood cells"]','C','Methaemoglobin contains Fe³⁺ instead of Fe²⁺, which cannot bind O₂. Furthermore, in tetramers with mixed Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺ subunits, the Fe²⁺ subunits show increased O₂ affinity (leftward shift), impairing O₂ release to tissues. Treatment is methylene blue (reduces Fe³⁺ back to Fe²⁺). Causes include nitrites, dapsone, and prilocaine.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Oxygen Transport','hard','mcq','During acclimatisation to high altitude, renal bicarbonate excretion sustains the ventilatory response to hypoxia by:',''["A. Increasing 2,3-DPG production", "B. Directly stimulating the carotid bodies via aldosterone", "C. Restoring pH toward normal despite low PCO\u2082, allowing peripheral chemoreceptors to respond fully to hypoxia", "D. Increasing haemoglobin synthesis through EPO"]','C','Acute hyperventilation at altitude lowers PCO₂ and raises pH (respiratory alkalosis), which dampens ventilatory drive. Over 2–4 days, kidneys excrete HCO₃⁻, restoring pH toward normal. This ''unlocks'' peripheral chemoreceptors to respond fully to the continuing hypoxic stimulus, sustaining elevated ventilation — the acclimatisation response.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','easy','mcq','The majority of CO₂ in blood is transported as:',''["A. Bicarbonate ions (HCO\u2083\u207b)", "B. Dissolved CO\u2082 in plasma", "C. Carbamino compounds bound to haemoglobin", "D. Carbonic acid (H\u2082CO\u2083)"]','A','Approximately 70% of CO₂ is transported as HCO₃⁻, formed when CO₂ enters RBCs and is hydrated by carbonic anhydrase to H₂CO₃, then dissociates to HCO₃⁻ + H⁺. About 23% is carried as carbamino-Hb, and ~7% is dissolved in plasma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','easy','mcq','Respiratory acidosis is characterised by:','["A. Decreased PaCO₂ and increased pH","B. Increased PaCO₂ and decreased pH","C. Decreased HCO₃⁻ and decreased pH with normal PaCO₂","D. Normal PaCO₂ with decreased pH"]','B','Respiratory acidosis results from CO₂ retention (hypoventilation), causing PaCO₂ to rise and pH to fall. Common causes: COPD exacerbation, neuromuscular weakness, opioid overdose. Renal compensation increases HCO₃⁻ reabsorption over days to partially restore pH.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','medium','mcq','The Haldane effect describes:',''["A. Decreased O\u2082 affinity of haemoglobin in acidic conditions", "B. Increased solubility of CO\u2082 in cold blood", "C. Increased CO\u2082-carrying capacity of deoxygenated haemoglobin compared with oxygenated haemoglobin", "D. The stimulatory effect of CO\u2082 on peripheral chemoreceptors"]','C','The Haldane effect: deoxygenated Hb carries more CO₂ than oxygenated Hb — both as bicarbonate (deoxy-Hb is a better buffer, generating less H⁺ per CO₂) and as carbamino-Hb (amino groups on deoxy-Hb bind CO₂ more readily). This aids CO₂ loading in peripheral tissues and unloading in pulmonary capillaries.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','medium','mcq','Carbonic anhydrase in red blood cells catalyses which reaction?',''["A. HCO\u2083\u207b + H\u207a \u2192 CO\u2082 + H\u2082O (one direction only)", "B. O\u2082 + CO\u2082 \u2192 carbonate + water", "C. Haemoglobin + CO\u2082 \u2192 carboxyhaemoglobin", "D. CO\u2082 + H\u2082O \u21cc H\u2082CO\u2083 \u21cc H\u207a + HCO\u2083\u207b"]','D','Carbonic anhydrase (CA) catalyses: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻. This reaction is bidirectional: in peripheral tissues (CO₂ → HCO₃⁻ for transport) and in pulmonary capillaries (HCO₃⁻ → CO₂ for exhalation). CA inhibitors like acetazolamide block this and cause metabolic acidosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','medium','mcq','The chloride shift (Hamburger phenomenon) occurs when:',''["A. Cl\u207b leaves RBCs in exchange for HCO\u2083\u207b entering during O\u2082 loading in lungs", "B. Na\u207a exchanges with HCO\u2083\u207b across the RBC membrane", "C. Cl\u207b enters RBCs in exchange for HCO\u2083\u207b exiting, maintaining electrical neutrality during CO\u2082 loading in tissues", "D. H\u207a ions shift from plasma into RBCs"]','C','In peripheral tissues, CO₂ → HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ inside RBCs. HCO₃⁻ exits the RBC via the AE1 (Band 3) anion exchanger and Cl⁻ enters to maintain electroneutrality — the chloride shift. In the lungs, the process reverses: Cl⁻ leaves and HCO₃⁻ re-enters RBCs to regenerate CO₂ for exhalation.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','medium','mcq','A patient hyperventilates due to anxiety, producing PaCO₂ of 25 mmHg. The expected pH is:',''["A. 7.20\u20137.25", "B. 7.35\u20137.45", "C. 7.65\u20137.70", "D. 7.50\u20137.60"]','D','Acute hyperventilation causes respiratory alkalosis. For each 10 mmHg acute fall in PaCO₂, pH rises by ~0.08 units. PaCO₂ falls from 40 to 25 mmHg (Δ15 mmHg), so pH rises by ~0.12 → pH ≈ 7.52. Symptoms include perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm from decreased ionised Ca²⁺.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','medium','mcq','In compensated chronic respiratory acidosis (e.g., stable COPD), expected blood gas findings include:',''["A. High PaCO\u2082, low pH, high HCO\u2083\u207b", "B. Normal PaCO\u2082, high HCO\u2083\u207b, high pH", "C. Low PaCO\u2082, low HCO\u2083\u207b, low pH", "D. High PaCO\u2082, near-normal pH, high HCO\u2083\u207b"]','D','In chronic respiratory acidosis, renal compensation increases HCO₃⁻ reabsorption by ~3.5 mEq/L per 10 mmHg rise in PaCO₂. pH moves toward (but rarely reaches) normal. Classic: PaCO₂ 60 mmHg, pH 7.34, HCO₃⁻ 34 mEq/L. This distinction is important before giving supplemental O₂ to COPD patients.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','hard','mcq','Using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation (pH = 6.1 + log[HCO₃⁻] / 0.03 × PCO₂), in a patient with pH 7.30 and PaCO₂ 52 mmHg, the approximate HCO₃⁻ is:',''["A. 12 mEq/L", "B. 18 mEq/L", "C. 30 mEq/L", "D. 24 mEq/L"]','D','7.30 = 6.1 + log([HCO₃⁻] / 0.03 × 52) → 1.20 = log([HCO₃⁻] / 1.56) → [HCO₃⁻] = 1.56 × 10^1.20 ≈ 1.56 × 15.85 ≈ 24.7 ≈ 24 mEq/L. This acute respiratory acidosis has a normal HCO₃⁻, indicating no significant renal compensation yet.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','hard','mcq','Acetazolamide is used prophylactically for altitude sickness. Its mechanism involves:',''["A. Stimulating peripheral chemoreceptors directly", "B. Increasing 2,3-DPG production in red blood cells", "C. Inhibiting renal carbonic anhydrase, causing metabolic acidosis that stimulates ventilation", "D. Dilating pulmonary arterioles to reduce hypoxic vasoconstriction"]','C','Acetazolamide inhibits carbonic anhydrase in renal tubules, reducing HCO₃⁻ reabsorption and causing mild metabolic acidosis (HCO₃⁻ loss). The resulting acidaemia stimulates central and peripheral chemoreceptors, increasing ventilation and raising PAO₂. This counteracts the respiratory alkalosis that blunts ventilatory drive at altitude.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','CO2 Transport & Acid-Base','hard','mcq','A patient with salicylate overdose has pH 7.48, PaCO₂ 28 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 18 mEq/L. This pattern represents:',''["A. Mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis occurring simultaneously", "B. Primary respiratory alkalosis with secondary metabolic alkalosis", "C. Primary metabolic acidosis with full respiratory compensation", "D. Primary metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation"]','A','Salicylate toxicity causes a mixed disorder: direct stimulation of the medullary respiratory centre causes respiratory alkalosis (low PCO₂, high pH tendency), while salicylate-induced uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation causes metabolic acidosis (low HCO₃⁻). Both processes coexist simultaneously — the net pH depends on which dominates.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','easy','mcq','In the upright lung, pulmonary blood flow (perfusion) is greatest at:',''["A. The base", "B. The apex", "C. The mid-zone", "D. Equally distributed throughout"]','A','In the upright position, gravity causes blood flow to be greatest at the lung bases (Zone 3) and least at the apices (Zone 1). Pulmonary arterial pressure (~25/8 mmHg) is relatively low, making gravity a dominant factor with a hydrostatic gradient of ~1 mmHg/cm height.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','easy','mcq','Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (HPV) is unique because it:',''["A. Causes vasodilation in response to low alveolar O\u2082", "B. Is mediated primarily by the sympathetic nervous system", "C. Occurs only in the systemic circulation", "D. Causes vasoconstriction in response to low alveolar O\u2082, diverting blood to better-ventilated regions"]','D','HPV is a local homeostatic reflex: low alveolar PO₂ (below ~70 mmHg) causes adjacent pulmonary arterioles to constrict, diverting blood to better-ventilated regions. This improves V/Q matching. It is mediated by inhibition of K⁺ channels → smooth muscle depolarisation → Ca²⁺ influx. This is the OPPOSITE of the systemic O₂ response.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','medium','mcq','In West''s Zone 1 (lung apex in upright position), flow characteristics are:',''["A. Pa > PA > Pv \u2014 arterial pressure exceeds alveolar, flow is continuous", "B. PA > Pa > Pv \u2014 alveolar pressure exceeds arterial pressure, capillaries are collapsed, creating alveolar dead space", "C. Pa > Pv > PA \u2014 all pressures exceed alveolar, flow is maximum", "D. PA = Pa = Pv \u2014 all pressures equal, flow is moderate"]','B','Zone 1 (apex): Alveolar pressure (PA) > Pulmonary arterial pressure (Pa) > Pulmonary venous pressure (Pv). When PA exceeds Pa, capillaries collapse and there is no flow — creating alveolar dead space. Zone 1 occurs normally at the apex and pathologically when Pa falls (haemorrhagic shock) or PA rises (high positive pressure ventilation).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','medium','mcq','The V/Q ratio at the lung apex in an upright individual is approximately:',''["A. 0.6", "B. 3.3", "C. 1.0", "D. 0.1"]','B','At the lung apex, ventilation is relatively preserved but perfusion is very low, giving a high V/Q ratio (~3.3). Apical alveoli have near-atmospheric composition: high PAO₂ (~130 mmHg) and low PACO₂ (~28 mmHg). The lung bases have lower V/Q (~0.6) due to greater perfusion relative to ventilation.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','medium','mcq','Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) differs from systemic vascular resistance (SVR) in that PVR is:','["A. 5–10 times higher than SVR","B. Much lower than SVR, reflecting the low-pressure high-flow pulmonary circulation","C. Regulated primarily by the sympathetic nervous system","D. Unchanged with increased cardiac output"]','B','PVR = (Mean PA pressure − LA pressure) / cardiac output = (15 − 5) / 5 = 2 Wood units. SVR ≈ (95 − 5) / 5 = 18 Wood units. PVR is ~6–10 times lower than SVR. PVR decreases further with increased cardiac output (capillary recruitment and distension) — the opposite of most tissues.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','medium','mcq','A V/Q ratio of 0 (zero) in a lung unit represents:',''["A. Ideal gas exchange", "B. True intrapulmonary shunt (perfusion without ventilation)", "C. Alveolar dead space (ventilation without perfusion)", "D. Normal V/Q mismatch"]','B','V/Q = 0 means perfusion present but no ventilation — blood passes through completely unventilated alveoli (e.g., atelectasis, consolidation, flooding with pus or oedema). Blood exiting these units has the same O₂/CO₂ as mixed venous blood and cannot be corrected by O₂ supplementation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','medium','mcq','Chronic hypoxia (e.g., severe COPD or living at high altitude) can lead to cor pulmonale. The sequence of events is:','["A. Hypoxia → pulmonary vasodilation → reduced PVR → right ventricular dilation","B. Hypoxia → hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction → pulmonary hypertension → right ventricular hypertrophy","C. Hypoxia → polycythaemia → reduced viscosity → reduced PVR","D. Hypoxia → systemic hypotension → compensatory right heart failure"]','B','Chronic hypoxia causes sustained hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction and subsequent pulmonary vascular remodelling (smooth muscle hypertrophy, intimal fibrosis), leading to pulmonary arterial hypertension. Elevated RV afterload causes right ventricular hypertrophy and eventual dilation — cor pulmonale.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','hard','mcq','In West''s Zone 3 (lung base), blood flow is determined by:',''["A. The difference between alveolar pressure and venous pressure (PA \u2212 Pv)", "B. Alveolar pressure alone", "C. Venous pressure minus arterial pressure", "D. The arterial-venous pressure gradient (Pa \u2212 Pv), independent of alveolar pressure"]','D','Zone 3 (base): Pa > Pv > PA. Both arterial and venous pressures exceed alveolar pressure, so capillaries remain open throughout the cardiac cycle. Flow is determined by the arterial-venous pressure gradient (Pa − Pv) — the classic Starling resistor. This zone has the greatest and most continuous blood flow.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','hard','mcq','A patient on high positive pressure mechanical ventilation (PEEP 20 cm H₂O) develops hypotension. The most likely respiratory mechanism is:',''["A. High alveolar pressure compresses pulmonary capillaries, increasing PVR and reducing cardiac output; elevated intrathoracic pressure also reduces venous return", "B. High alveolar pressure expands Zone 3 and increases venous return", "C. PEEP causes widespread hypoxic vasoconstriction", "D. PEEP increases FRC, worsening V/Q mismatch"]','A','High PEEP raises mean intrathoracic pressure, impeding venous return (reducing preload) and compressing pulmonary capillaries (increasing PVR and RV afterload). This reduces cardiac output and blood pressure. It also converts more lung to Zone 1/2 (PEEP-generated alveolar pressure exceeds arterial pressure), increasing alveolar dead space.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','V/Q Matching & Pulmonary Blood Flow','hard','mcq','In a patient with pulmonary embolism occluding the right lower lobe pulmonary artery, which immediate V/Q change occurs in the affected lung zone?','["A. V/Q ratio falls toward 0 (shunt effect)","B. V/Q ratio rises toward infinity (dead space effect)","C. V/Q ratio remains 0.8 due to compensatory redistribution","D. Hypoxic vasoconstriction reduces ventilation to match lost perfusion"]','B','Pulmonary embolism blocks perfusion (Q → 0) while ventilation of that unit continues (V remains). V/Q rises toward infinity — creating alveolar dead space. The embolised lung units ventilate but cannot participate in gas exchange, increasing physiological dead space and (if significant) raising PaCO₂. Hypoxaemia results from V/Q mismatch in remaining perfused lung units.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','easy','mcq','The primary generator of the respiratory rhythm (breathing pacemaker) is located in:',''["A. The dorsal respiratory group/pre-B\u00f6tzinger complex in the medulla", "B. The pneumotaxic centre in the upper pons", "C. The apneustic centre in the lower pons", "D. The cerebral cortex"]','A','The dorsal respiratory group (DRG) in the nucleus tractus solitarius, specifically the pre-Bötzinger complex, generates the basic inspiratory rhythm. It produces rhythmic burst discharges driving each breath. Pontine centres modulate this rhythm but are not essential for its generation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','easy','mcq','Peripheral chemoreceptors that respond to low PaO₂ are located in the:','["A. Medullary ventral surface","B. Carotid and aortic bodies","C. Pulmonary stretch receptors","D. J-receptors in alveolar walls"]','B','The carotid bodies (at the bifurcation of the common carotid artery) and aortic bodies (near the aortic arch) are peripheral chemoreceptors. They respond to decreased PaO₂ (below ~60 mmHg), increased PaCO₂, and decreased pH. The carotid bodies are quantitatively more important in humans (transmit via CN IX, glossopharyngeal nerve).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','medium','mcq','Central chemoreceptors in the medulla respond primarily to:',''["A. Changes in arterial PO\u2082", "B. Changes in arterial pH directly", "C. Pulmonary stretch receptor signals", "D. Changes in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) pH, reflecting arterial PCO\u2082"]','D','Central chemoreceptors on the ventral medullary surface are surrounded by CSF. CO₂ crosses the blood-brain barrier readily; it hydrates (via carbonic anhydrase) to H₂CO₃ → H⁺ + HCO₃⁻, lowering CSF pH. The receptors respond to this pH change (effectively sensing PCO₂). They account for ~70% of the ventilatory response to CO₂ and do NOT respond to PO₂.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','medium','mcq','The pneumotaxic centre in the upper pons primarily functions to:',''["A. Limit the duration of inspiration, thereby increasing respiratory rate", "B. Generate the basic inspiratory rhythm", "C. Prolong inspiration to allow maximum tidal volumes", "D. Respond directly to blood PO\u2082 changes"]','A','The pneumotaxic centre (nucleus parabrachialis, upper pons) sends continuous inhibitory signals to the inspiratory area, switching off inspiration and controlling inspiratory duration and respiratory rate. Without pneumotaxic input, inspiration is prolonged (apneusis), reducing respiratory rate and increasing tidal volume.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','medium','mcq','In long-standing COPD with chronic CO₂ retention, the primary stimulus for breathing becomes:','["A. Elevated PaCO₂ stimulating central chemoreceptors","B. Low PaO₂ stimulating peripheral chemoreceptors (hypoxic drive)","C. Low pH stimulating central chemoreceptors directly","D. Reduced lung compliance stimulating J-receptors"]','B','With chronic CO₂ retention, central chemoreceptors reset to the elevated PCO₂ baseline. CSF bicarbonate rises to normalise CSF pH, blunting the central CO₂ response. The patient relies mainly on peripheral hypoxic drive (low PaO₂). This is why excessive supplemental O₂ can paradoxically suppress ventilation in these patients and worsen CO₂ retention.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','medium','mcq','The Hering-Breuer reflex prevents over-inflation of the lungs. The receptors responsible are:',''["A. Slowly adapting pulmonary stretch receptors in airway smooth muscle, transmitting via the vagus nerve", "B. Carotid body chemoreceptors", "C. J-receptors (juxtacapillary receptors) in alveolar walls", "D. Irritant receptors in the tracheal mucosa"]','A','Slowly adapting pulmonary stretch receptors (SARs) in airway smooth muscle detect lung inflation. During large tidal breaths, they send inhibitory signals via the vagus nerve (CN X) to the inspiratory centre, terminating inspiration (inspiratory off-switch). The Hering-Breuer reflex is prominent in infants and anaesthetised adults, less so in conscious adults during normal breathing.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','medium','mcq','Which of the following is the strongest stimulus for increasing alveolar ventilation?',''["A. A fall in PaO\u2082 from 100 to 80 mmHg", "B. A fall in pH from 7.40 to 7.35 with normal PCO\u2082", "C. A rise in PaCO\u2082 from 40 to 50 mmHg", "D. An increase in body temperature by 1\u00b0C"]','C','CO₂/H⁺ is the most powerful stimulus for ventilation. An acute 10 mmHg rise in PaCO₂ approximately doubles alveolar ventilation. PaO₂ must fall below ~60 mmHg before peripheral chemoreceptors significantly stimulate ventilation. The CO₂ response is mediated by central (~70%) and peripheral (~30%) chemoreceptors.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','hard','mcq','Damage to the apneustic centre in the lower pons results in:',''["A. Complete apnoea", "B. Cheyne-Stokes breathing", "C. Increased respiratory rate with normal tidal volume", "D. Apneusis \u2014 prolonged inspiratory gasps with brief expirations"]','D','The apneustic centre promotes sustained inspiration. Normally the pneumotaxic centre overrides it. If the pneumotaxic centre is damaged AND vagal afferents (Hering-Breuer) are cut, the apneustic centre drives prolonged sustained inspiration (apneusis) interrupted by brief expirations. This pattern can occur with lower pontine strokes.','universal',false,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','hard','mcq','Hyperventilation before a breath-hold dive increases breath-hold time but is dangerous because:',''["A. Hyperventilation raises PaO\u2082, providing a larger O\u2082 reserve but causes alkalosis", "B. Hyperventilation increases lung compliance, reducing effort of breath-holding", "C. Hyperventilation alkalinises blood, sensitising chemoreceptors to O\u2082", "D. Hyperventilation lowers PaCO\u2082, delaying the CO\u2082 urge to breathe, but O\u2082 may fall to unconscious levels before PaCO\u2082 rises sufficiently to force breathing (shallow water blackout)"]','D','Hyperventilation lowers PaCO₂ significantly below normal, delaying the CO₂-stimulated urge to breathe. However, O₂ stores are only marginally increased (flat part of O₂-Hb curve). During breath-hold, O₂ falls to dangerously low levels (PaO₂ 30–40 mmHg) before PaCO₂ rises enough to force breathing — the diver may lose consciousness without warning (shallow water blackout), a life-threatening phenomenon.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Control of Breathing','hard','mcq','Congenital central hypoventilation syndrome (Ondine''s curse) results from PHOX2B mutations. Breathing is characterised by:','["A. Normal automatic breathing with inability to breathe voluntarily","B. Normal voluntary breathing but failure of automatic metabolic respiratory control during sleep","C. Increased ventilatory response to CO₂ with periodic hyperventilation","D. Episodic bronchospasm due to vagal hyperactivity"]','B','PHOX2B mutations disrupt development of chemoreceptor neurons and the automatic respiratory control system. Patients breathe normally during wakefulness (voluntary cortical control intact) but hypoventilate or apnoeae during sleep (when automatic metabolic control is required). They require nocturnal ventilatory support (e.g., home mechanical ventilation or diaphragm pacing).','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','easy','mcq','During moderate aerobic exercise, ventilation increases primarily because of:',''["A. A significant rise in PaCO\u2082 stimulating central chemoreceptors", "B. A significant fall in PaO\u2082", "C. Neural signals from exercising muscles and joints (neurogenic drive), with CO\u2082 precisely matching ventilation to metabolism", "D. Adrenaline release from the adrenal medulla"]','C','At the onset of exercise, ventilation increases almost instantaneously due to cortical irradiation and proprioceptive signals from muscles and joints — before any blood gas changes occur. PaCO₂, PaO₂, and pH remain near-normal during moderate exercise because ventilation increases proportionally to CO₂ production.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','easy','mcq','Acclimatisation to high altitude over several days involves which adaptive changes?','["A. Decreased erythropoietin and lower haemoglobin concentration","B. Increased erythropoietin, polycythaemia, increased 2,3-DPG, and sustained hyperventilation","C. Decreased 2,3-DPG to improve O₂ loading onto haemoglobin","D. Bronchodilation to reduce airway resistance"]','B','Altitude acclimatisation: (1) acute hyperventilation; (2) renal HCO₃⁻ excretion restoring pH to allow sustained ventilation; (3) increased EPO → polycythaemia (weeks); (4) increased 2,3-DPG (rightward shift aids tissue O₂ delivery); (5) increased pulmonary diffusing capacity. These collectively improve O₂ delivery at reduced PAO₂.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','medium','mcq','During strenuous exercise beyond the anaerobic threshold, ventilation increases disproportionately because:','["A. PaCO₂ falls significantly, stimulating central chemoreceptors","B. Lactic acidosis adds H⁺ ions that stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors, causing excess ventilation above metabolic CO₂ production","C. Core temperature directly activates the pneumotaxic centre","D. Diaphragmatic fatigue receptors increase rate"]','B','Above the anaerobic (lactate) threshold, lactic acid accumulates and H⁺ ions stimulate peripheral and central chemoreceptors in addition to the normal CO₂ drive. This produces disproportionate ventilation increase (VE/VCO₂ rises), causing PaCO₂ to fall below normal — respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','medium','mcq','Acute exposure to very high altitude (above 5500 m) produces which immediate respiratory change?',''["A. Hyperventilation causing respiratory alkalosis, driven by peripheral chemoreceptor response to low PaO\u2082", "B. Hypoventilation due to suppressed peripheral chemoreceptors", "C. Increased PaCO\u2082 as CO\u2082 production rises with hypoxia", "D. Decreased respiratory rate with increased tidal volume"]','A','Acute high altitude exposure lowers PAO₂ and PaO₂. When PaO₂ falls below ~60 mmHg, carotid body peripheral chemoreceptors are strongly activated, causing hyperventilation. This reduces PaCO₂ (respiratory alkalosis). The resulting alkalosis partially dampens the drive, but peripheral hypoxic stimulation maintains elevated ventilation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','medium','mcq','Obstructive sleep apnoea (OSA) is characterised by:',''["A. Central apnoea with no respiratory effort", "B. Reduced tidal volume without upper airway obstruction", "C. Paradoxical increase in ventilation during REM sleep", "D. Repetitive upper airway collapse causing cessation of airflow despite continued respiratory effort"]','D','OSA is caused by upper airway muscle hypotonia during sleep (especially pharyngeal dilators), causing repetitive pharyngeal collapse. Continued inspiratory effort against the closed airway generates large negative intrathoracic pressures without airflow (obstructive apnoeas), terminating with arousal. This is distinguished from central apnoea where respiratory effort ceases entirely.','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','medium','mcq','Cheyne-Stokes breathing is characterised by:',''["A. Rapid shallow breathing at a fixed rate", "B. Prolonged inspiration followed by rapid expiration", "C. Increased tidal volume without change in respiratory rate", "D. Cyclic crescendo-decrescendo breathing with periods of apnoea, caused by oscillation in the ventilatory control system"]','D','Cheyne-Stokes breathing occurs due to instability in the respiratory control loop — most commonly when circulation time from lungs to brain is prolonged (e.g., severe heart failure), causing delayed and oscillating CO₂ signals. Also seen in CNS lesions, uraemia, and high altitude (where hypocapnia from hyperventilation periodically silences breathing).','universal',true,'{year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','medium','mcq','High altitude pulmonary oedema (HAPE) is caused primarily by:',''["A. Alveolar hypoventilation causing CO\u2082 retention and fluid shift", "B. Decreased serum albumin causing low oncotic pressure", "C. Non-uniform hypoxic vasoconstriction with regions of high-pressure blood flow causing pulmonary capillary stress failure", "D. Increased pleural pressure compressing alveoli"]','C','HAPE pathophysiology: non-uniform HPV diverts blood to regions with less vasoconstriction, which then experience high blood flow and high capillary hydrostatic pressure. This leads to mechanical capillary injury (stress failure) and high-protein oedema. Risk factors include rapid ascent and physical exertion at altitude.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','hard','mcq','During REM sleep, breathing becomes irregular because:',''["A. Chemoreceptor sensitivity is markedly increased during REM sleep", "B. Upper airway muscle tone is preserved but cortical respiratory drive is reduced", "C. Tidal volume increases by 50% during REM due to diaphragmatic hyperstimulation", "D. The automatic medullary breathing centres are suppressed; breathing becomes driven by variable cortical input, with reduced skeletal muscle tone making it irregular"]','D','During REM sleep, the medullary respiratory centres are relatively suppressed and breathing becomes irregular — resembling voluntary waking patterns. Skeletal (intercostal and accessory) muscle tone is markedly reduced (REM atonia), so breathing depends mainly on the diaphragm. Patients with neuromuscular disease or severe COPD are most vulnerable to respiratory failure during REM sleep.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','hard','mcq','At maximal exercise in a trained athlete, O₂ delivery (VO₂max up to 4–5 L/min) is primarily maintained by:',''["A. Increased haemoglobin concentration by splenic contraction alone", "B. Activation of anaerobic metabolism as the dominant pathway", "C. Increased cardiac output (up to 25\u201330 L/min), wider arteriovenous O\u2082 difference, and increased pulmonary diffusing capacity", "D. Increased respiratory rate only, with unchanged cardiac output"]','C','VO₂max depends primarily on: (1) cardiac output (rises from 5 to 25+ L/min in athletes); (2) increased O₂ extraction (C(a-v)O₂ widens from ~5 to ~15 ml/dl); (3) increased DLCO (capillary recruitment). Ventilation rises to 100–150 L/min but is rarely the limiting factor in healthy individuals; cardiac output is the primary constraint.','universal',true,'{year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),,
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Respiratory System','Anatomy & Physiology','Respiratory Responses','hard','mcq','A mountaineer at 8000 m breathing ambient air (PB ≈ 267 mmHg) without supplemental O₂, with hyperventilation reducing PaCO₂ to 12 mmHg, would have an estimated PAO₂ of approximately:',''["A. 100 mmHg", "B. 31 mmHg", "C. 60 mmHg", "D. 15 mmHg"]','B','PiO₂ = 0.21 × (267 − 47) = 0.21 × 220 = 46.2 mmHg. PAO₂ = 46.2 − (12/0.8) = 46.2 − 15 = 31.2 ≈ 31 mmHg. Actual measurements on the summit of Everest (8849 m) show PAO₂ ≈ 28–35 mmHg — near the level of loss of consciousness without acclimatisation. Only extreme acclimatisation allows survival.','universal',true,'{year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology 14th Ed')
